<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Roman Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">753 BCE – 509 BCE (approx.) · capital Rome</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rome under its seven kings, traditionally 753–509 BCE, when a Latin settlement on the Tiber became a city with temples, a forum and institutions that outlived the monarchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> villages on the Palatine and nearby hills by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> crossing. Tradition dated its founding by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> to 753 BCE; archaeology shows settlement from the 10th century BCE and the drainage and paving of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Forum</a> around 650–600 BCE, when the villages became a single city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king (rex) ruled with the advice of a Senate of clan elders, and the people were organised in thirty curiae. Tradition credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> religious institutions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servius-tullius">Servius Tullius</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and a new army based on wealth classes. The last three kings had Etruscan links; under them Rome built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloaca-maxima">Cloaca Maxima</a> and the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-god">Jupiter</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>, and became the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tradition says that after Sextus Tarquinius raped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia">Lucretia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> led the nobles in expelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a> in 509 BCE. The kingship was replaced by two annually elected consuls, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. The title rex survived only in a priesthood, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> long hated the very word.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/roman-kingdom.jpg" alt="Roman Kingdom" width="320" height="311"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Filipvr at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Platner_-_Ancient_Rome_city_growth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>753 BCE – 509 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rome</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Numa Pompilius, Servius Tullius, Tarquinius Superbus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regal Rome, Kingdom of Rome</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-631&amp;focus=roman-kingdom" aria-label="Roman Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 686 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Roman Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 942L5304 942M5304 942L5308 947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5308 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rome. <a href="/atlas?year=-631&amp;focus=roman-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
